{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: raise the personal allowance to £10,000; protect NHS frontline services and integrate health/social care; £2.5bn Pupil Premium and a plan to phase out university tuition fees; identify £15bn in savings for credible deficit reduction; targeted jobs and green‑growth stimulus; firm‑but‑fair immigration (exit checks, enforcement, earned citizenship); clean‑up politics (fair votes, recall, fixed terms). TEXT_G: leave the EU as the core mission; five‑year immigration freeze and caps; 40% increase in defence spending and renew Trident; a single 31% flat income tax and abolish Inheritance Tax when possible; repeal the Climate Change Act; replace 70+ benefits with a flat Basic Cash Benefit; widespread voucher schemes and direct‑democracy reforms.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top concerns in this period centered on the economy and deficit, cost of living/tax, the NHS and schools, plus cleaner politics and controlled immigration, rather than EU withdrawal or large defence expansions. TEXT_D addresses these mainstream priorities directly with tax cuts for low/middle earners, NHS and education-focused measures, credible savings, and measured immigration reform, while TEXT_G concentrates on EU exit and more radical overhauls less aligned with broad voter priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
